{"reference":"Job 37","book":"Job","chapter":37,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"In the Larger Dialogue","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Job 37 concludes Elihu’s four uninterrupted speeches, delivered after Job and the three friends had stopped speaking. Elihu says he had held back because he was younger and is angry with both Job and the friends. His place in the book remains debated: some readers see his speeches as preparing for God’s answer, while others see him as another inadequate defender of retribution. The narrative’s silence about Elihu in the opening, God’s speech, and the closing rebuke does not by itself settle the question.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_037_b6e5041caa9a0f14dd66fc4e"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Across the whole story, Job is sometimes remembered for endurance or steadfastness, including in connection with suffering and the Lord’s compassionate outcome. That description can refer to Job’s persistence and to the story’s outcome; it should not be read as requiring silent acceptance of abuse or as preventing escape and protection.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_037_5c35a465bfd400bfe2d9579b"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}